<p>Corpus Christi mold damage claims almost always start with water. A pipe bursts behind a wall, a roof opens up in a storm, an AC drain line overflows in the attic, or floodwater sits in a home for days. In the warm, humid Coastal Bend climate, mold can begin growing quickly on wet drywall, insulation, carpet pad, and wood framing. Insurance coverage for mold is often narrow and is usually tied to the water loss that caused it. If you own a home or business in Corpus Christi, Nueces County, or nearby communities, this page explains how mold claims generally work, where they are commonly limited, and how a public adjuster may help.</p>

<h2>How Corpus Christi Mold Damage Claims Are Usually Handled</h2>
<p>Most property policies do not treat mold as a stand-alone peril. Instead, many policies cover mold, if at all, only when it results from a covered water loss, and many of those policies cap mold with a sublimit. Some policies exclude mold entirely, while others add back limited coverage by endorsement. That means the first questions on any mold claim are usually:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>What caused the water?</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a storm-created roof opening is treated very differently from a slow leak or flood.</li>
<li><strong>Is the underlying water loss covered?</strong> If the water damage itself is excluded, the mold usually is too.</li>
<li><strong>What does the policy say about mold?</strong> Look for an exclusion, a sublimit, or a mold endorsement.</li>
<li><strong>Was the water addressed promptly?</strong> Many policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page explains the underlying water claim in more detail.</p>

