<h2>Why Mold Is Common After Coastal Bend Losses</h2>
<p>Local conditions make fast drying especially important. Long, hot summers and humid Gulf air slow natural drying, and many homes are slab-on-grade with drywall and baseboards close to the floor, so even a modest leak can wick up walls. After a hurricane or widespread storm, power outages and contractor backlogs can delay drying for days. During the February 2021 winter storm, when Corpus Christi International Airport recorded a low of 17&deg;F on February 15, many homes had split pipes at the same time, and mitigation crews were stretched thin. Delays like that can turn a water claim into a mold claim. Our article on <a href="/freeze-damage-burst-pipe-claims-corpus-christi/">freeze damage and burst pipe claims</a> covers that kind of event.</p>

<h2>Documenting a Mold Claim</h2>
<p>Because mold coverage is tied so closely to the water loss, documentation needs to cover both:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The water source</strong> &ndash; photos of the failed pipe, appliance, or roof opening, plus a plumber or roofer report.</li>
<li><strong>The timeline</strong> &ndash; when the water event happened, when you discovered it, when you reported it, and when drying began.</li>
<li><strong>Mitigation records</strong> &ndash; extraction and drying invoices, moisture readings, and photos of removed materials.</li>
<li><strong>Visible mold</strong> &ndash; photos and video of affected areas, including inside wall cavities once opened.</li>
<li><strong>Assessment and protocol</strong> &ndash; a mold assessment and written remediation protocol, if one is done, to define the scope.</li>
<li><strong>Contents</strong> &ndash; a list of affected items such as upholstered furniture, mattresses, and clothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you organize the file.</p>

<h2>Where Mold Claims Get Disputed</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cause.</strong> The insurer says the mold came from long-term seepage, humidity, or maintenance issues rather than a covered event.</li>
<li><strong>Timing.</strong> The insurer argues the loss was reported late or drying started too late.</li>
<li><strong>Sublimits.</strong> Remediation, testing, and rebuild costs may all be counted against a mold sublimit, which can be used up quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Scope.</strong> The estimate may allow cleaning where removal and replacement is needed, or leave out containment, air scrubbing, and clearance testing.</li>
<li><strong>Separation of costs.</strong> Costs that belong to the covered water damage, such as replacing water-damaged drywall, may be wrongly lumped into the mold sublimit.</li>
</ul>
<p>That last point matters. On many claims, it is worth separating ordinary water damage repairs from mold-specific work so that each is measured against the right part of the policy. An <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can help identify how costs were allocated.</p>

<p>Timing rules still apply. For many insurers, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 requires acknowledging a claim within 15 days of notice and accepting or rejecting it within 15 business days after receiving the items requested (<a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tex. Ins. Code Ch. 542</a>). Reporting the underlying water loss early starts that process sooner.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the policyholder only. On a mold-related claim, that may include reviewing the policy for mold exclusions, sublimits, and endorsements; documenting the underlying water loss and timeline; reviewing assessment and remediation records; preparing a detailed estimate that separates water damage repairs from mold remediation where appropriate; and communicating with the insurer's adjuster. We serve Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities.</p>
<p>We cannot promise coverage or any outcome; mold claims depend heavily on policy language and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>What to Do If You Find Mold</h2>
<ol>
<li>Find and stop the water source if you can.</li>
<li>Report the underlying water loss to your insurer promptly.</li>
<li>Photograph the mold and the water damage before anything is removed.</li>
<li>Start drying, and keep affected rooms closed off where practical.</li>
<li>Use qualified mold professionals for assessment and remediation.</li>
<li>Keep every invoice, report, and moisture reading.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you have health concerns, talk with a medical professional. For questions about a denied claim, a lawsuit, or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
